\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Standard number systems are denoted by $\mathbb N,\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$; index ranges, computational models and specialized notation follow the cited problem source and the definitions below.


Degree-$d$ SOS search seeks a certificate using polynomial squares and constraint multipliers within the prescribed degree bound. Coefficient bounds concern exact encoding size, separately from the number of monomials or certificate degree.

Polynomial time means a number of elementary steps bounded by a polynomial in the encoded input length. A decision problem is in $\mathrm{NP}$ if yes-instances have polynomial-length certificates verifiable in polynomial time; NP-hardness is under polynomial-time reductions.

A polynomial is a sum of squares (SOS) if it is $\sum_j q_j^2$ for polynomials $q_j$. Moment-SOS hierarchies impose positivity through increasingly large truncated moment matrices and corresponding degree-bounded SOS certificates.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Characterize meaningful conditions under which low-degree sum-of-squares proofs can be found in polynomial time with polynomially bounded coefficients. In particular, determine when the degree-$d$ sum-of-squares relaxation is algorithmically computable in time polynomial in the natural input size for fixed $d$. \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2025/9}{[S2]}
\paragraph{Scope qualification.}
The input coefficient model, precision, strict-feasibility promises, and allowed constraint multipliers are those of the source; fixed degree alone is not asserted to ensure efficient exact computation.
\paragraph{Source status.}
The supplied catalogue labels this record partially solved. Its source-status metadata was checked against the supplied snapshot on 2026-10-11. This import does not independently establish current open status or mathematical correctness. The catalogue literature assessment is dated 2026-08-22.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Find conditions making low-degree sum-of-squares proof search polynomial-time with manageable coefficients.

